Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 01:51:09 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 21/39] Steady-state detection: line hooks for the fused sweep

A sinusoidal excitation adds the steady-state detection (Engine_Ext_SteadyState), and that extension had
no line hooks, so the multithreaded engine ran every such simulation (all cases of upstream's benchmark,
benchmark/cases.py) on the unfused plan. Apply2VoltagesLines() now records the E-field probes of its
x-lines and, on an evaluation step, copies the voltages and currents of its lines; the thread that copies
the last lines evaluates the period from the copies with the same energy sum (Engine_sse::FastEnergy,
split out of CalcFastEnergy). The unfused hook sees the voltages after their update and the currents
before theirs, and so do the copies, line by line: same values, same operations, same stop step
(bit-identical). The copies (24 B per cell) are allocated at the first evaluation step of a fused run.

Verified on boxes with Mur, PML, lumped RLC, conducting sheets, Drude and Debye blocks, a delayed and a
soft H excitation (sinusoidal, 3000-30000 steps): raw fields, the E-field probe and a trace of every
period evaluation (step, energy, change) identical to the sse-compressed engine at 1, 3 and 4 threads,
fused and unfused; the stop step and probe identical to stock v0.37.0. Upstream's cases (benchmark/cases.py,
1 M cells, 3000 steps, fused against OPENEMS_FUSED=0 in the same build, paired interleaved A/B, 5 rounds,
other processes on about half a core): pml x1.36 at 4 threads (at 8 within the noise of a run under 2-3
cores of other load), inhomogeneous x1.29 at 4 and x1.34 at 8,
ideal x0.98 at 4 and x0.80 at 8 (a homogeneous box, where the unfused plan's work stealing evens out the
threads within a timestep; the neighbour flags later recover most of it). The dispersive case needs the
Debye line hooks of the next commit as well.

